# The Contract

Every unit you have read so far — [[Enrich]], [[Score]], the [[Pipeline]] — has a
**contract**: the promise of what it offers, what it needs, and the shapes that
cross its edge. The surprising part of the work model is that you have never
*written* a contract. You wrote Elixir. The contract is the Elixir module, read a
certain way:

- the module's **public functions are its exports** — `enrich/1`, `score/1` are
  what other units may call;
- its **dependencies and grants are its imports** — `grant net: "api.crm.com"` is
  the one capability :enrich asks the host to fulfill;
- its **structs, atoms, and tuples are its types** — `%Lead{}`, the `@statuses`
  enum, the `{:ok, lead} | {:error, reason}` variant;
- a shared `@behaviour` is an **interface** — the thing a kit declares so many
  units can satisfy one shape.

That is the whole idea, and it is worth stating plainly before anything else: the
contract is not a separate artifact you maintain in lockstep with the code. It IS
the code. Learn Elixir, not a contract language.

## Reading a module as a contract

Take a unit small enough to hold in your head. The point is to *read the same
lines two ways* — once as ordinary Elixir, once as a contract.

server :tax, in: :pricing do
  @moduledoc """
  Compute sales tax for a quote. This module's surface IS its contract:
  one export (tax/2), the imports it asks for live on the sandbox edge,
  and the types are the structs and tuples that cross the boundary.
  """

  # An export. Because it's a public def, other units may call it; the weave
  # gate records `:tax → tax/2` as an edge in the code graph. Its inferred type
  # is (Money.t(), region :: atom()) -> {:ok, Money.t()} | {:error, :no_rate}.
  def tax(%Money{cents: cents} = subtotal, region) do
    case rate_for(region) do
      {:ok, bps} ->
        added = div(cents * bps, 10_000)
        {:ok, %Money{subtotal | cents: cents + added}}

      :miss ->
        {:error, :no_rate}
    end
  end

  # A private def. NOT an export — it is invisible at the boundary, so it never
  # appears in the contract. Internal refactors here change nothing for callers.
  defp rate_for(region) do
    case Map.fetch(@rates, region) do
      {:ok, bps} -> {:ok, bps}
      :error -> :miss
    end
  end

  # A type, declared as structure. No DSL, no @type lead-in — the defstruct's
  # default values infer the field types (cents is an integer here).
  defmodule Money do
    defstruct cents: 0, currency: :usd
  end

  @rates %{us: 0, ca: 725, ny: 800}
end

Read it as code and it is a tax function over a `Money` struct. Read it as a
contract and it says: *I export `tax/2`; I take a `Money` and a region atom; I
return a tagged variant; I live inside the [[Pricing]] sandbox, so my imports are
whatever that boundary grants.* Same lines. You never wrote the second reading;
it was always there.

## An interface is a `@behaviour`

When several units must agree on one shape — the definition of a kit — the shared
contract is an Elixir `@behaviour`. The callbacks are the agreed exports; each
implementer's module is checked against them at the weave gate.

defmodule Scorer do
  @moduledoc """
  The interface every scoring unit satisfies. This behaviour IS the kit's
  contract: any module that adopts it must export these two functions with
  these shapes, or the weave gate refuses the bundle.
  """

  # An export every implementer must provide. The callback's signature is the
  # contract; :score (the canonical unit) satisfies it, and so could a tenant's
  # own scorer dropped in beside it.
  @callback score(Lead.t()) :: integer()

  # A second required export: explain why, for the dashboard tooltip.
  @callback explain(Lead.t()) :: String.t()
end

A unit adopts it with `@behaviour Scorer` and the compiler holds it to the shape.
This is how [[FinModel]] is shipped as a kit and how a customer can supply their
own `:score` without forking ours: the interface is the contract, the
implementation is theirs.

## The Dock: one seam for every import

A contract names imports; *something* has to fulfill them. That something is the
**Dock** — the single capability seam every unit talks through. There is exactly
one membrane, and every privileged act — a network fetch, a key read, a database
query, a file open — is a **request** that crosses it:

> **request → membrane → provider**

The membrane checks the request against the calling unit's grants. If the grant
covers it, the membrane routes the request to a **provider**, of which there are
two kinds:

- `local` — fulfilled on the device: the OS through Tauri on the desktop, or the
  browser's own APIs on the web.
- `runtime` — fulfilled by the shared server tier (the **nexus**) over RCP.

The same line of unit code — `Dock.fetch(url)` in [[Enrich]] — is satisfied by a
different provider depending on where the workbook is delivered, and the unit
never knows which. That is the point of a single seam: the unit declares the
*need*, the Dock decides the *fulfillment*.

server :dock_demo, grant: [net: "api.crm.com", kv: :secrets] do
  @moduledoc """
  Nothing here calls a provider directly. Each Dock.* call is a request that
  hits the membrane; the membrane checks our two grants and routes to local
  or runtime. The unit is provider-blind on purpose.
  """

  def lookup_account(name) do
    # A network request. Membrane checks `net: "api.crm.com"` against the host,
    # then routes: on the nexus this goes out over the server's egress; in a
    # desktop build it could be the OS network stack. Same call either way.
    key = Dock.secret(:crm_api_key)
    url = "https://api.crm.com/v1/accounts?name=" <> URI.encode(name)

    Dock.fetch(url, headers: [{"authorization", "Bearer " <> key}])
  end

  # A key read. `Dock.secret/1` is itself a request across the membrane; it is
  # only fulfilled because we hold `kv: :secrets`. A unit without that grant
  # asking for the key gets refused at the seam, not handed an empty string.
  # That refusal IS the boundary doing its job.
end

Grants are how a contract and the Dock meet: the contract lists the imports; the
Dock is the one place those imports are honored or denied. A unit cannot reach
around the seam, which is why a client island can be hydrated in an untrusted
browser and still be safe — it simply has no grant the membrane will honor for a
secret or an arbitrary host.

## The Nexus: the optional server tier

The `runtime` provider has a name: the **nexus**. It is the optional server tier
behind the Dock, and it is where the trusted, heavy, and long-lived work lives:

- **agents** — the analyst, coder, researcher, and reflexion loops run their
  steps in native server Elixir, with typed `data` as their memory;
- **data and sync** — the canonical store and the diffs that drive live SSR;
- **the compiler lane** — turning a `client svelte` island or a Rust/Zig unit
  into wasm;
- **multi-tenancy and isolation** — keeping one customer's run off another's, the
  nexus's job rather than any single wasm box's.

A workbook *can* ship with no nexus at all — the static bundle hydrates entirely
in the browser, and any `runtime`-only request simply has no provider, so units
that need one are inert. Add a nexus and the same workbook gains agents, sync,
and server SSR **without a rewrite**. The contract didn't change; a second
provider showed up behind the seam to fulfill the imports the static delivery
left unfulfilled.

server :nexus_note do
  @moduledoc """
  A unit that only makes sense with a nexus. In a static bundle its runtime
  requests have no provider, so it stays inert; deliver via the nexus and the
  same code runs, agents and all. No branch in the unit decides this — the Dock
  does, by which providers are present.
  """

  def draft_outreach(%Lead{} = lead) do
    # Asks the nexus to run the analyst agent. This is a runtime-only request:
    # the membrane has nowhere to route it in a static bundle, and routes it to
    # the agent loop when a nexus is attached. The unit code is identical.
    Dock.agent(:analyst, lead: lead, ask: "draft a first-touch email")
  end
end

## The generated WIT — shown once, never written

Everything above compiles to a standard **wasm component** (the Component Model),
which is how an Elixir unit, a Rust [[Forecast]] kernel, and a Python [[Risk]]
scorer can all interoperate and all be sandboxed. The component's contract is
expressed, at that low level, as a **WIT world**. You are about to see one. It is
the *only* place in this entire workbook a WIT artifact appears, and the reason it
appears is to convince you that you will never write it.

This world is **generated** from the `:tax` module above — the public `tax/2`
became an export, the `Money` struct and the region atom became types, the
sandbox's net grant became an import. Read it once, recognize that every line maps
back to Elixir you already wrote, and then forget it.

// GENERATED — you never touch this. Emitted by the weave from the :tax module.
// Each line below corresponds to Elixir you already wrote, above.
package sales:tax@1.0.0;

world tax {
  // from `defstruct cents:, currency:` — the inferred Money type
  record money {
    cents: s64,
    currency: string,
  }

  // from the `{:ok, Money} | {:error, :no_rate}` tagged variant
  variant tax-result {
    ok(money),
    err(tax-error),
  }
  enum tax-error { no-rate }

  // from the public `def tax(%Money{}, region)` — the one export
  export tax: func(subtotal: money, region: string) -> tax-result;

  // from the `:pricing` sandbox grant — the one import the Dock must fulfill
  import host:net/fetch;
}

That file is real, correct, and machine-made. You did not author a single line of
it; the weave derived all of it from the module. If a future maintainer ever opens
the generated world to "fix" the contract, they are editing the wrong thing — the
fix belongs in the Elixir, and the world is regenerated from it. **The rule holds:
learn Elixir, not WIT.**

## Why this matters for the rest of the app

Because the contract is the module, the **code graph** can be built straight from
your source: public defs become export nodes, grants become import edges, structs
and variants become type nodes, `@behaviour` becomes an interface edge, and the
`[[backlinks]]` and `:atom` references in this very prose become ref edges. The
weave gate walks that graph to resolve references, audit that every import has a
grant, and run the tests — and a red test ships no bundle.

So when [[Pipeline]] calls :enrich and then :score, the gate already knows the
shapes line up, the net grant is accounted for, and the `:enriched` lead that
leaves one unit is exactly what the next one's contract accepts. None of that
needed a contract language. It needed only the modules you already wrote, read as
the contracts they always were.
